[FINAL-TEXT] I have all the evidence needed. The log tail reveals the precise failure moment and mechanism.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: spine_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The FRR suite on spine_router_0_0 has crashed and the `frr.service` is now `inactive (dead)`. Specifically, the `bgpd` daemon process exited at 2026-08-04 19:04:57, roughly 88 seconds after all three BGP sessions had fully converged and were exchanging keepalives normally. The FRR log on spine_router_0_0 records the exact failure moment: zebra logged "Client 'bgp' (session id 1) encountered an error and is shutting down" followed immediately by all three bgp client disconnections. When bgpd exited, zebra tore down its RIB entries and spine_router_0_0 stopped responding to TCP port 179 — causing all three peers (super_spine_router_0 toward 172.16.0.1, leaf_router_0_0 toward 172.16.0.4, leaf_router_0_1 toward 172.16.0.6) to simultaneously transition to BGP `Active` state, exactly as reported in the alarm.

Data-plane traffic between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1 is unaffected because spine_router_0_1 is still up and its BGP sessions to both leaves and super_spine are fully established, providing alternate paths; the fabric is simply running single-spine.

The BGP configuration in `/etc/frr/frr.conf` is intact and correct (AS 65100, router-id 172.16.0.1, three eBGP neighbors). There is no configuration error. The root cause is the bgpd process crashing and taking the entire frr.service down with it.

Corrective fix: SSH to spine_router_0_0 and run `systemctl start frr`. BGP will reload from the existing `/etc/frr/frr.conf`, re-establish all three sessions, and full fabric redundancy will be restored within one hold-time interval. Investigate kernel logs or coredumps for the underlying bgpd crash cause to prevent recurrence.
=== END ===